CH = dict(num=13, tag="Maths (Part 2)", theme="Mean as a balance point, effect of changes on mean and median, frequency tables, spreadsheets, line graphs",
 title="Tales by Dots and Lines",
 intro="The mean is the balance point of data, and the median is its middle. This chapter shows how both react when values are added, removed or changed, how to find missing values, how to use frequency tables and spreadsheet formulae, and how to read line graphs and infographics sensibly.",
 big_questions=["How does the mean behave as a balance point, and what happens when the data changes?", "How do we find a mean or median from a frequency table or a spreadsheet?", "How do we read and interpret line graphs and other data displays?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "The Mean as a Balancing Point"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Mean (average)** = sum of all values ÷ number of values. The **median** is the middle value of the data arranged in order (the average of the two middle values if the number of values is even)."),
 ("ul", ["On a dot plot, the mean is the point where the data **balances**: the **total distance** of the values below the mean equals the **total distance** of the values above it.", "The mean of two numbers is exactly halfway between them (3 and 7 give 5).", "The mean is **not** always the midpoint of the smallest and largest values. For 10, 10, 11, 17 the mean is 12, but the midpoint of 10 and 17 is 13.5.", "There is only **one** such balance point: shifting it in either direction makes one side's distance larger than the other's."]),
 ("h2", "What Happens to the Mean When Data Changes?"),
 ("table", ["Change", "Effect on the mean", "Reason"], [["Include a value **greater** than the mean", "Mean increases", "More distance on the upper side, so the balance point moves up"], ["Include a value **smaller** than the mean", "Mean decreases", "Balance point moves down"], ["Include or remove a value **equal** to the mean", "Mean stays the same", "It adds no distance on either side"], ["Remove a value greater than the mean", "Mean decreases", "Upper-side distance shrinks"], ["Add a fixed number k to **every** value", "Mean increases by k", "New mean = a + k"], ["Multiply **every** value by k", "Mean is multiplied by k", "New mean = k × a"]]),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "If x₁, x₂, … xₙ have mean **a**, then the values x₁ + k, … xₙ + k have mean **a + k**, and the values kx₁, … kxₙ have mean **k × a**."),
 ("p", "**Proof for adding k.** New mean = [(x₁ + k) + (x₂ + k) + … + (xₙ + k)] ÷ n = (x₁ + x₂ + … + xₙ + nk) ÷ n = a + k. **Check:** the data 4, 6, 7, 7, 9 has mean 6.6. Adding 10 to every value gives mean 16.6; doubling every value gives mean 13.2."),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Several values can be added together without changing the mean if their distances from the mean cancel out: for example, two values below the mean and one above it, chosen so that the total distance below equals the distance above."),
 ("h2", "What Happens to the Median?"),
 ("ul", ["Including a value **greater** than the median makes the median **increase** (or stay the same); including a smaller value makes it decrease (or stay the same).", "Example: for 5, 6, 8, 8, 9, 10 the median is 8. Including 11 gives seven values and the median becomes the 4th value, 8; including two large values moves the middle to the right.", "The median depends on the **position** of values, so extreme values change it much less than they change the mean."]),
 ("h2", "Finding an Unknown Value"),
 ("steps", [("Step 1", "Use total = mean × number of values."), ("Step 2", "Add the known values."), ("Step 3", "Unknown value = total − sum of the known values.")]),
 ("p", "**Example.** The mean of 8, 10, 12, 15, 9 and one smudged number is 12. Total of six values = 12 × 6 = 72. The known five add up to 54, so the missing value is 72 − 54 = **18**."),
 ("p", "**Correcting a mean.** A shopkeeper finds the mean daily sale over 20 days is ₹18.5 (in thousands), so the total is 370. One day was entered 5 too high, so the correct total is 365 and the correct mean is 365 ÷ 20 = **18.25**. We did not need the individual values."),
 ("h2", "Mean and Median from a Frequency Table"),
 ("p", "When a value occurs several times, multiply it by its **frequency** before adding. Do not average the distinct values alone."),
 ("table", ["Family size", "3", "4", "5", "6"], [["Frequency (students)", "2", "5", "8", "5"], ["Value × frequency", "6", "20", "40", "30"]]),
 ("ul", ["Number of values = 2 + 5 + 8 + 5 = **20**; sum = 6 + 20 + 40 + 30 = **96**.", "Mean = 96 ÷ 20 = **4.8**.", "Median: the 10th and 11th values are the middle. Cumulative frequencies are 2, 7, 15, so both lie in the group of 5. Median = **5**."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "To find the median from a frequency table, add the frequencies **cumulatively** from the smallest value until you reach the middle position. You never have to write out all the values."),
 ("h2", "Spreadsheets"),
 ("p", "A **spreadsheet** is a grid of **cells** arranged in **columns** (A, B, C, …) and **rows** (1, 2, 3, …). A cell is named by its column letter and row number, so E5 is column E, row 5."),
 ("table", ["Formula", "What it does"], [["=SUM(B3:G3)", "Adds the marks in cells B3 to G3 (one student's total across subjects)"], ["=AVERAGE(D2:D6)", "Mean of the first five students' marks in column D"], ["=AVERAGE(G2:G23)", "Class average of a column of 22 students"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Start:End** (for example B3:G3) names a block of cells in one row or column.", "A formula always begins with **=**.", "Change one number and every formula that uses it updates automatically."]),
 ("h2", "Line Graphs"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **line graph** joins data points with line segments. It is best for showing how a quantity **changes over time**, for example the monthly maximum temperature of a state."),
 ("steps", [("Step 1 — Identify what is given", "Read the title, the axes, the scale, the units and the legend (colours or marker shapes for each line)."), ("Step 2 — Infer and interpret", "Describe the trend (rising, falling, flat), find peaks and low points, compare lines, and note the steepness: a steeper segment means a faster change."), ("Step 3 — Question the data", "Ask how the data was collected, what is missing, and what can and cannot be concluded.")]),
 ("ul", ["A clustered column graph with 13 years × 4 countries would need 52 bars and look crowded; a line graph shows the same trend clearly.", "Monthly *averages* (for example rainfall) are obtained by averaging the same month over several years.", "Different shaped markers help readers who print in black and white or find colours hard to distinguish.", "Use a line graph for trends over time and a bar graph for comparing separate categories."]),
 ("h3", "Example reading"),
 ("p", "If a line for City A rises from 20 to 38 between January and June and then falls, while City B stays between 29 and 33 all year, we can say City A's temperature varies much more than City B's. The graph tells us what happens, not why; the reasons need further questions and data."),
 ("h2", "Other Ways of Showing Data"),
 ("ul", ["**Infographic:** combines colours, maps and symbols to compare data at a glance, for example a map shaded by how strongly each state prefers rice or wheat. Read the colour scale first.", "**Time strip:** a strip of 48 boxes (one for each half hour from midnight to midnight) coloured by activity; it shows how a day is spent.", "**Data story:** a graph plus questions, such as how daily sleep changes with age; a smooth curve is used because it has many closely spaced points."]),
 ("h2", "Common Mistakes"),
 ("ul", ["Averaging only the distinct values of a frequency table instead of multiplying by frequencies.", "Taking the mean to be the midpoint of the smallest and largest values.", "Forgetting that the median needs the data to be **sorted** first.", "Reading a line graph without checking the scale or where the axis starts."]),
 ("qa", [("Why is the mean called a balance point?", "The total distance of the values below it equals the total distance of the values above it."), ("What happens to the mean of a set if every value increases by 5?", "The mean also increases by 5."), ("What happens to the mean if a value equal to the mean is added?", "It does not change."), ("The mean of 5 numbers is 14. Four of them add up to 58. What is the fifth?", "Total = 70, so the fifth number is 70 − 58 = 12."), ("What does =AVERAGE(C2:C10) compute?", "The mean of the nine numbers in cells C2 to C10.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Mean = sum ÷ number of values; it is the balance point where distances on both sides are equal.", "Adding a value above the mean raises it, below the mean lowers it, equal to the mean leaves it unchanged.", "Adding k to every value adds k to the mean; multiplying every value by k multiplies the mean by k.", "Missing values: total = mean × number of values, then subtract the known values.", "For a frequency table, mean = sum of (value × frequency) ÷ total frequency; find the median by cumulative frequency.", "Spreadsheet cells are named by column letter and row number; =SUM(…) and =AVERAGE(…) work on ranges like B3:G3.", "Read line graphs in two steps: first what is given (axes, scale, legend), then what it means; line graphs suit trends over time."])
